# DATABASE_URL=postgres://postgres:postgres@localhost/chat
DATABASE_URL=postgres://xhui@localhost/chat



# sqlx migrate run
# pgcli chat
# \dt
# select * from _sqlx_migrations


# The .env file you are looking at is not explicitly called by any line of code (like dotenv::dotenv()) inside your Rust source code! Instead, it is being used implicitly by sqlx tools and the Rust compiler.

# In a standard sqlx project like this, the .env file serves two main purposes:

# 1. By the sqlx-cli (Command Line Tool)
# When you type terminal commands like:

# sqlx migrate run
# sqlx database create
# The sqlx command-line tool automatically looks for a .env file in the current directory. It reads the DATABASE_URL variable so it knows exactly which database to apply the migrations to.

# 2. By the Rust Compiler (Compile-time Checks)
# This is the most "magical" part of sqlx. If your code uses macros like sqlx::query! or sqlx::query_as!, the Rust compiler itself reads the .env file while it is compiling your project to find DATABASE_URL.
# It actually connects to your live database during compilation to statically verify that your SQL syntax is valid and your table schemas match your Rust structs!

# Summary
# Is it read at runtime when the server runs? No, not unless you install a crate like dotenvy and explicitly tell your code to parse it. Right now your chat_server uses an app.yml file for runtime configuration instead.
# Who reads it? The sqlx tools and the Rust macro system read it while you are writing, building, migrating, or compiling the project.



# # Why `postgres://xhui@localhost/chat` Works Without a Password

# The answer depends on how PostgreSQL is configured on your Mac.

# ## PostgreSQL Authentication Methods

# PostgreSQL supports multiple authentication methods. Your system is likely using **one of these**:

# ### 1. **Trust Authentication** (Most Likely)
# In `pg_hba.conf`, your local connections might be set to `trust`:

# ```
# # PostgreSQL Host-Based Authentication (pg_hba.conf)
# local   all             all                                     trust
# host    127.0.0.1/32    all                                     trust
# ```

# This means: **"Allow any user to connect from localhost without a password"**

# So `postgres://xhui@localhost/chat` works because PostgreSQL trusts connections from your local machine.

# ### 2. **Peer Authentication** (Unix Domain Socket)
# If you're connecting via Unix socket (not TCP), PostgreSQL uses the OS username:

# ```
# local   all             all                                     peer
# ```

# Since your OS user is `xhui`, PostgreSQL allows you to connect as the `xhui` database user without a password.

# ### 3. **No Password Set for `xhui` User**
# You might have created the `xhui` database user without a password:

# ```bash
# createuser xhui  # No password
# ```

# vs.

# ```bash
# createuser xhui --password  # Prompts for password
# ```

# ## Check Your Configuration

# To see your actual authentication settings:

# ```bash
# # Find pg_hba.conf
# sudo find /opt/homebrew -name pg_hba.conf 2>/dev/null
# # or
# sudo find /usr/local -name pg_hba.conf 2>/dev/null

# # View it
# cat /path/to/pg_hba.conf | grep -A 5 "local"
# ```

# ## Why Those Other Formats Don't Work

# - `postgres://xhui:postgres@localhost/chat` — Expects password `postgres` for user `xhui`, but no password is set
# - `postgres://postgres:xhui@localhost/chat` — Expects user `postgres` with password `xhui`, but authentication is likely set to `trust` (no password needed)

# ## Summary

# Your setup works because PostgreSQL is configured with **trust authentication for localhost**, which means:
# - ✅ Any user can connect from `localhost` without a password
# - ✅ Your connection string `postgres://xhui@localhost/chat` is sufficient
# - ✅ No password credentials needed for local development





# # Understanding Your PostgreSQL Setup

# Perfect! Your `pg_hba.conf` confirms it:

# ```
# local   all             all                                     trust
# host    all             all             127.0.0.1/32            trust
# host    all             all             ::1/128                 trust
# ```

# ## What This Means

# | Rule | Applies To | Authentication |
# |------|-----------|-----------------|
# | `local all all trust` | Unix socket connections | **No password needed** |
# | `host all all 127.0.0.1/32 trust` | TCP from localhost (IPv4) | **No password needed** |
# | `host all all ::1/128 trust` | TCP from localhost (IPv6) | **No password needed** |

# ## Why Your .env Works

# ```
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://xhui@localhost/chat
# ```

# ✅ **Works because:**
# - You're connecting to `127.0.0.1` (localhost over TCP)
# - The rule `host all all 127.0.0.1/32 trust` applies
# - `trust` method means PostgreSQL accepts any user without a password
# - No `:password` needed in the connection string

# ## Why The Others Don't Work

# ❌ `postgres://xhui:postgres@localhost/chat`
# - Even though you provide a password, PostgreSQL ignores it (trust method doesn't validate passwords)
# - But the connection string format becomes semantically odd

# ❌ `postgres://postgres:xhui@localhost/chat`
# - Same issue: PostgreSQL doesn't check the password due to `trust`

# ## The .env Purpose in Your Project

# As noted in your file comments:

# 1. **sqlx-cli reads it** when you run:
#    ```bash
#    sqlx migrate run
#    sqlx database create
#    ```

# 2. **Rust compiler reads it** during build-time macro expansion:
#    ```rust
#    sqlx::query!("SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ?")
#    // ↑ Compiler connects to DB to verify schema at compile time
#    ```

# 3. **Runtime**: Your `chat_server` uses `app.yml` instead (not .env)

# Your setup is correct for local development! 🎯